-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Ray Origin Inside Sphere:** If $\mathbf{o}$ is inside the sphere, $C < 0$, resulting in one negative and one positive root. The positive root is the exit point. If the caller expects an entry point, this must be handled explicitly [S5, S6, unofficial].
-*   **Precision Errors:** When the ray is tangent ($\Delta \approx 0$), floating-point errors can cause $\Delta$ to be slightly negative, missing the hit. A small epsilon tolerance may be applied to $\Delta$ [S6, G, unofficial].
-*   **Uninitialized Variables:** Incorrect implementation may reference uninitialized $t$ when computing the normal before assigning it [S6, unofficial].
-*   **Self-Intersection:** A threshold (e.g., $0.0001$) is often added to $t_{min}$ to prevent the ray from intersecting the same object it just hit (z-fighting) [S6, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Ray Origin Inside Sphere**: If $\mathbf{o}$ is inside the sphere, $C < 0$. The discriminant is positive, and one root will be negative (exit behind) and one positive (exit forward). The logic above handles this by selecting the smallest $t \ge t_{min}$. If $t_{min}=0$, it returns the exit point. For volume marching, both entry and exit are often needed; if the ray starts inside, entry is effectively $t_{min}$ or the negative root [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Precision Errors**: When the ray is tangent ($\Delta \approx 0$), floating-point errors can cause $\Delta$ to be slightly negative, resulting in a miss. A small epsilon tolerance may be applied to $\Delta$ [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Uninitialized Variables**: Incorrect implementations may reference the intersection distance $t$ before it is assigned when computing the normal, leading to garbage values [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Direction Normalization**: If $\mathbf{d}$ is not normalized, $A \neq 1$. The quadratic formula must use the computed $A$ [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### 3-Sphere Intersection (Trilateration)
-Finds the intersection points of the surfaces of three spheres, used to generate new sample points on the boundary of a carved volume [S9]. It computes $T(\mathcal{S}_k) = \{\partial \mathcal{S}_a \cap \partial \mathcal{S}_b \cap \partial \mathcal{S}_c, (\mathcal{S}_a, \mathcal{S}_b, \mathcal{S}_c) \in \mathcal{S}_k^3\}$, representing the intersection of boundaries of three distinct spheres [S9]. This is used in sphere carving algorithms to ensure convergence by placing new spheres on existing surface intersections [S9].
+### [3-Sphere Intersection (Trilateration)](5144-3-sphere-intersection-trilateration.md)
+Computes the geometric points where the surfaces of three distinct spheres intersect. Used in sphere carving to generate new sample points on the boundary of a carved volume. It solves a system of three quadratic equations rather than a single ray-sphere intersection.
+
+### [Sphere Intersection](1620-sphere-intersection.md)
+General term for finding points on a sphere satisfying specific constraints. In the context of this family, it often refers to point-sphere distance checks or other geometric queries distinct from the ray-parametric intersection.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ constant time. Requires a few dot products, a square root, and basic arithmetic [S5, S8, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Extremely fast; often the fastest primitive intersection test [S8, unofficial]. The square root is the most expensive operation, but unavoidable for exact analytical solutions [S8, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Exact solution, no iterative approximation, robust for convex shapes [S5, S8, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Only applies to spheres. Does not handle non-uniform scaling or rotation without transformation overhead [S1, unofficial]. Precision issues near tangency [S6, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When the scene contains spherical objects or bounding volumes [S5, unofficial]. When exact intersection is required [S8, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** For complex meshes or non-spherical primitives [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$. The method involves a fixed number of arithmetic operations (dot products, square root) regardless of scene size [S5, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$. Requires storage for a few temporary vectors and scalars [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: Extremely fast. It is one of the fastest primitive intersection tests available, making it suitable for GPU execution in parallel threads [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Analytical solution (no iteration), robust, simple to implement [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Only applicable to spherical primitives. For complex volumes, the sphere must approximate the volume (e.g., bounding sphere), which can lead to overestimation of the marching distance and wasted samples if the sphere is much larger than the actual volume [S5, S7, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use**: When the volume or object is spherical or can be tightly bounded by a sphere. Ideal for early-outs in acceleration structures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use**: For non-spherical primitives or when tighter bounds are required (use [Ray-box intersection](776-ray-box-intersection.md) or [Ray-Tetrahedron Intersection](2525-ray-tetrahedron-intersection.md)).
 
 ## Background
-The analytical ray-sphere intersection is a fundamental technique in ray tracing, dating back to the early days of computer graphics [S1, S8, unofficial]. It is derived from the parametric equation of a line and the implicit equation of a sphere [S5, unofficial]. It is widely used in volume rendering to determine entry and exit points for marching algorithms [S5, unofficial], and in general ray tracing for sphere primitives [S6, S8, unofficial]. Variants exist for transformed spheres [S1, unofficial] and for use in signed distance field (SDF) ray marching [S8, unofficial].
+The analytical ray-sphere intersection is a fundamental technique in computer graphics, dating back to the early days of ray tracing. It is derived from substituting the parametric line equation into the implicit equation of a sphere. It is widely used in volume rendering for determining marching bounds [S5, unofficial] and in path tracing for shading [S1, S3, unofficial]. The method is often implemented in local space to simplify calculations, but world-space direct solving is common in modern GPU implementations to avoid transformation overhead [S1, S5, unofficial].
 
